WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.400
If I talk about a European island that’s
divided in two, a former British colony where

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.790
you drive on the left and whose economy has
grown substantially in recent years, what

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.840
country would you think about?

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.900
I’m sure many would say... Ireland!

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:23.900
Right?

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.310
Well...sorry we won’t be looking at the
country of Guinness, the Harlings and green

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.610
leprechauns this time.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.160
There’s another island, which isn’t talked
about much, that also fits that description.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.710
Dear friends of VisualPolitik, in this video
we’re going to talk about Cyprus, a Mediterranean

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:42.270
island that is much hotter than Ireland but
which is also a territory split in two.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:50.750
You see, this story isn’t that well-known.On
the island of Cyprus we can find two … different

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:51.750
states:

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.340
The Republic of Cyprus and the self-proclaimed
Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus, which

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.320
is only recognized as a sovereign country
by Turkey.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.129
So, while the southern part is fully integrated
within the European Union, the northern part

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:06.259
only interacts with Turkey’s political and
commercial networks.

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:12.110
So, as you can imagine, both sides have huge
political and economic differences.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:16.619
Now, surely many of you may be wondering...

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:19.510
But how did this small Mediterranean island
become divided?

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.119
What is the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus?

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:26.840
We’ll answer these and many other questions
in this video.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.479
So…

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:31.759
Listen up.

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:37.289
(CYPRUS, A CASUALTY OF THE GREAT EMPIRE)

00:01:37.289 --> 00:01:39.830
This isn’t a unique case.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:45.149
In many of the colonies of the former British
Empire, decolonization brought a lot of ethnic

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:46.900
and territorial problems.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.180
This case is just another example.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.580
Cyprus was granted to the United Kingdom as
a protectorate following an agreement with

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.460
the Ottoman Empire, at the end of the 19th
century.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.569
It was given control of the island in gratitude
for the support the British had given to the

00:02:02.569 --> 00:02:04.560
Ottomans in their war against Russia.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.489
However, it wasn’t until the beginning of
World War I, in 1914, that the United Kingdom

00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:12.910
formally declared Cyprus a colony.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.590
Now, how was this colonial territory?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:16.590
How did it end?

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.260
How did it affect Cypriot history?

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.659
Well... the truth is that the colonization
of Cyprus wasn’t plain sailing.

00:02:25.659 --> 00:02:30.849
You see, many Cypriots, especially those of
Greek ethnicity, utterly rejected colonial

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:35.340
rule, and now that they had been freed from
the Ottoman Empire, they wanted to annex the

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.439
island to Greece, something known as Enosis.

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:45.140
And as you can imagine, the Cypriots of Turkish
origin didn’t like this idea much, as they

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.810
didn’t identify with Greece at all.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.379
We’re talking about a confrontation that
fueled several revolts, which in some cases

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:53.450
had to be stifled by British forces.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:03:01.299
Now, the biggest problem, the final confrontation
happened after they gained independence in

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:04.739
1960.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:09.209
The confrontation for power between the Greek
community and the Turkish community escalated

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:15.969
to such a point that just three years after
independence, in 1963, violence broke out.

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:21.609
This situation led Turkey to threaten military
intervention in order to protect Turkish Cypriots.

00:03:21.609 --> 00:03:29.659
Finally, the United States managed to defuse
the situation, but the battle had just begun.

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:34.989
After years of intermittent instability and
tense calm, finally in 1974, a controlled

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:40.139
coup d'etat from Athens overthrew Archbishop
Makarios III’s government and triggered,

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:43.639
just five days later, Turkey’s invasion
of Northern Cyprus.

00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:49.870
And so, that’s how a division was drawn
which continues to this day, and which was

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:55.340
formally recognised in 1983 with the unilateral
creation of the State of the Turkish Republic

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.760
of Northern Cyprus, a country of just 300,000
inhabitants, which is only recognized by Turkey

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:09.150
and that 36 years after its independence doesn’t
even have a national anthem.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:14.469
It also explains why, in the capital city
of this island, Nicosia, we can find a Berlin-style

00:04:14.469 --> 00:04:16.870
wall that divides the city in two.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:17.870
But...

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.340
Would you like to know how Cyprus handles
this national division today, and what remains

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.770
of the past tensions?

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:27.970
What would we find if we went to Nicosia,
Cyprus’s capital right now?

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.600
Well...

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.850
Listen up...

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:39.210
(NICOSIA, EUROPE’S LAST DIVIDED CAPITAL?)

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.610
Walking through some areas of Nicosia today
is the closest thing to walking through old

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.740
divided Berlin.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:53.340
In many streets, especially those of the old
walled city, you can see barriers, fences

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.139
and walls made with barrels filled with concrete.

00:04:57.139 --> 00:05:04.900
It’s the separation between the Greek Cypriot
and the Turkish Cypriot Nicosia.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.710
A separation that has a completely abandoned
demilitarized strip that is controlled by

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.900
the UN, known as the Green Line.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.800
(MAP)

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:21.259
This line extends from end to end across the
island, and is a Korean-style demilitarized

00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:25.481
zone, with a maximum width of 7 kilometers,
and which accounts for 3% of the island's

00:05:25.481 --> 00:05:28.000
entire territory.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:35.900
But it’s in the capital, Nicosia, where
the Green Line is most apparent.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:40.030
It is there that the worst clashes between
both communities took place during the second

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.370
half of the 20th century.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:49.970
To give you an idea, in the capital, time
seems to have stopped along the strip.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:57.110
And as a testament of that convulsive past,
you can see, for example, the former airport.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:04.310
A facility that was completely abandoned in
1974 – yes, since the coup and the subsequent

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:09.169
Turkish invasion.

00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:13.810
Now the only function of this airport is to
serve as a backdrop when a few tourists decide

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.599
to pose for pictures in the old waiting rooms
and in front of the corroded sixties-style

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:23.710
check-in desks, which seem to have been leftover
from an apocalyptic movie.

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:27.430
Of course, for those who love dark tourism,
it’s a “must see”, because you can get

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.430
some impressive images from that place.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.130
And that's not all.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:39.919
In the old workshops and car dealerships of
the Green Line in Nicosia, you can see old

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:43.539
imported and abandoned models that nobody
has touched since 1974.

00:06:43.539 --> 00:06:47.941
There are also bars, restaurants and abandoned
houses where you can still see old bottles

00:06:47.941 --> 00:06:54.720
of Coca-Cola on the table and gunshot blasts
on the facades.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:59.940
But, we aren’t just talking about an abandoned
strip of land.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.650
Even though the tensions have remained dormant
in recent years, the issue goes much deeper.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:10.460
Allow me to explain, despite being in the
Mediterranean, the Turkish Republic of Northern

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.570
Cyprus is one of the most isolated places
on earth.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:17.759
It hasn’t been recognized by any countries
and has been internationally sanctioned for

00:07:17.759 --> 00:07:21.330
the past four decades.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.440
From an international point of view, its only
access is Turkey, its inhabitants lack their

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.290
own passport and, any establishment with international
brands and franchises is prohibited.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:35.840
(AUDIO: “At all levels, economically, socially
and politically, it’s as if we didn’t

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:36.840
exist.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:41.229
[...] We live in a country where regular contact
with the rest of the world is severely compromised.”

00:07:41.229 --> 00:07:47.020
Ozdil Nami, special Turkish Cypriot representative
in the negotiations for resolving the Cyprus

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:48.020
dispute)

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:52.111
In fact, it wasn’t until 2003, when some
restrictions were relieved, that the green

00:07:52.111 --> 00:07:56.139
line was crossed for the first time in more
than 30 years.

00:07:56.139 --> 00:08:00.060
Now, at this point, the question that crops
up is...

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.879
What can be done to resolve this?

00:08:01.879 --> 00:08:06.189
Could this division end soon?

00:08:06.189 --> 00:08:07.979
Well...

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:10.560
If you look at the history, it seems hard.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.050
Even with the help of the Secretary General
of the United Nations who is determined to

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.729
achieve reunification.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:24.470
António Guterres has set himself the challenge
of achieving an agreement to reunify Cyprus

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.900
under a bi-communal and bi-zonal federal system.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:32.300
And to do so, 2020 could either be a turning
point or just another year on the already

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:36.740
long list of Cyprus’s diplomatic failures.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:41.099
For now, the UN has set itself the objective
of resuming negotiations in mid-2020.

00:08:41.099 --> 00:08:47.810
(AUDIO: UN hails 'frank' Cyprus talks, vows
to seek peace effort restart - Al Jazeera)

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:53.820
However, in recent years a new player has
emerged, further complicating the issue.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.980
Listen up.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.850
(REUNIFICATION, AN IMPOSSIBLE ROAD?)

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:05.490
When anything breaks, putting it back together
is very complicated.

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:09.860
And, of course, if we’re talking about countries,
it can be an almost impossible mission.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.350
That’s exactly what’s happening in Cyprus.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:17.400
You see, even though the tensions between
both communities have greatly decreased, there

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.030
are still difficult barriers to overcome.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:25.620
What will happen to the Turkish troops, considering
that Turkish Cypriots basically depend on

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:26.620
them?

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:29.890
How will the issue of the properties seized
from Greek Cypriot owners be resolved?

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.880
How will both communities be politically organized
so that one isn’t under the other’s control?

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.630
How will the territory be distributed?

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.280
What will happen with social policy?

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.200
Will the south finance the development of
services and infrastructure in the much poorer

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.020
north?

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:50.620
And with all these questions comes a new geostrategic
problem, one that threatens to kill these

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.830
negotiations and which has a lot to do with
something we’ve already talked about here

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:58.210
on this channel.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:02.340
Cyprus is currently involved at a commercial
and economic turning point that Turkey doesn’t

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:03.340
approve of.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:07.839
And even though the Turkish Republic of Northern
Cyprus is, in theory, an independent country,

00:10:07.839 --> 00:10:13.420
the truth is that Turkey rules Northern Cyprus’
interests by aligning them with theirs, especially

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:15.970
when it comes to international politics.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:23.840
After all, it’s the only country that recognizes
this republic, while also maintaining more

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.460
than 30,000 soldiers deployed on the island.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:30.840
The fact is that it’s here that we face
a new obstacle, in the shape of a long, thick

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.700
tube located under the sea.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.000
We’re talking about the EastMed gas pipeline.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:43.150
The EastMed is a pipeline whose construction
could begin very soon, which will allow Israel

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:46.870
to export natural gas directly to Europe by
2025.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:53.870
Israel... and Cyprus, which has also begun
to exploit its gas reserves at sea.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:11:00.470
And, as we’ve already discussed in a previous
video, Israel has managed to boost its gas

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.000
production, achieving very significant production
figures.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:10.160
In fact, just a short time ago, on January
2nd, 2020, they took a giant step regarding

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:11.160
this project.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:17.160
(AUDIO: Leaders From Israel, Cyprus, Greece
Sign EastMed Gas Pipe Deal - Bloomberg)

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:21.970
Now, the question is, why could this be a
problem when it comes to conversations about

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:24.010
Cypriot reunification?

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:30.180
Well, because this clashes directly with the
geostrategic and economic interests of Turkey,

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:34.680
which is where the gas pipeline that currently
conducts exported gas from the Middle East

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:36.070
to Europe flows.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:41.020
This situation gives Turkey the power to control
the passage of a vital supply to a continent

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:45.050
that hasn’t been very friendly to the Turkish
regime in recent years.

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:49.140
You can imagine how this goes, right?

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:52.870
Well, if we add to this mix the fact that
it’s Israel, ISRAEL, who would benefit the

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.900
most from this project, then things get even
more complicated.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:12:02.450
Because, lately Turkey and Israel are getting
on worse than Batman and the Joker.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:08.230
So, if reunification was already a challenge,
this new confrontation between Turkey and

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:11.860
the Republic of Cyprus only looks to make
it much more complicated.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.510
Anyway, this is how things stand in Cyprus
today.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:18.350
But now it's your turn: Did you know about
the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus?

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:22.830
Do you think these two states should reunify
or should they maintain their independence?

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:24.380
Leave your answer in the comments.

